Title: The River Keeps
Credit: Reenactment scenes
Author: Lowwater Films
Draft date: October 2026
Contact: Fictional sample script for the Open Filmmaker Toolkit

[[Fictional sample. The people, places and events are made up.]]

EXT. RIVERSIDE LEVEE - NIGHT #1#

= 1952. Neighbors fill sandbags as the river rises.

Rain in sheets. Lanterns swing along the top of the levee. A line of NEIGHBORS passes sandbags hand to hand.

EDITH HAMMOND, 34, a scarf over her hair, works the shovel at the sand pile. Her husband WALT, 36, carries two bags at a time.

WALT
That's the last of the sand from the yard.

EDITH
Then we dig the garden.

MR. PRYOR, 60s, holds a lantern over the water line painted on a post.

MR. PRYOR
(quietly)
It's past the mark. Up a foot since supper.

Edith looks at the black water, then back toward town.

EDITH
Josie's alone at the house.

WALT
Go. I'll stay with the line.

INT. HAMMOND HOUSE - KITCHEN - NIGHT #2#

= Edith writes the first diary entry by lamplight.

A kerosene lamp on the table. JOSIE, 9, asleep on the bench under a coat. Edith, soaked, opens a small cloth-bound diary.

EDITH (V.O.)
April the ninth. The river is over the mark at Pryor's post. Walt is on the levee with the others. I have moved the flour and the photographs upstairs.

She stops writing. Listens. Rain on the roof, and under it, a low rushing.

JOSIE
(waking)
Is it coming in?

EDITH
Not tonight. Go back to sleep.

EXT. GRAIN ELEVATOR - DAWN #3#

= The men watch the water from the loading dock.

Gray light. Water to the second rung of the loading ladder. Walt and Mr. Pryor stand on the dock with a few other MEN, mud to the knees.

MR. PRYOR
The south levee went at four.

WALT
And ours?

MR. PRYOR
Holding. For now.

A rowboat drifts past, empty, turning slowly in the current.

INT. HAMMOND HOUSE - ATTIC - DAY #4#

= Edith and Josie carry what they can save up the attic stairs.

Edith hands a crate of jars up the narrow stairs to Josie. Water stands an inch deep on the floor below.

JOSIE
What about the piano?

EDITH
The piano can swim.

Josie laughs despite herself. Edith sets the diary on the top beam, where it will stay dry.

EXT. RIVERSIDE LEVEE - DAY #5#

= After the water goes down, the neighbors walk the levee.

Sun. Mud everywhere. The sandbag wall stands, slumped but whole. Edith, Walt and Josie walk the top of the levee with the NEIGHBORS.

Mr. Pryor paints a new line on his post, a foot above the old one.

MR. PRYOR
For the next ones.

EDITH (V.O.)
April the fourteenth. The levee held. We are all of us tired, and all of us here.

FADE OUT.
